+## 7. Finding 6 — why this is BLOCKED, and the exact minimal patch
+
+The boundary implemented is `ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft()`: it now decides the complete
+set of buffers for **one buffer type** before allocating any of them. That is a genuine transaction,
+but it is not whole-load.
+
+`llama_model::load_tensors()` (`src/llama-model.cpp:1748`) iterates `ml.ctx_map` and calls that
+function once per `(buft, ctx)` pair. Each completed call has already allocated before the next
+context is even planned, so a load that fails on the third buffer type leaves the first two placed.
+No existing llama or ggml entry point hands any backend the full cross-buffer-type set, and the
+in-load size census does not exist: the size list is computed *inside*
+`ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft_impl()` and is not exposed. llama's `no_alloc` memory-fit
+pass is a separate whole model-load invocation driven from `llama_model_params`, not a dry pass
+usable inside a load.
+
+Closing this requires a larger loader transaction. Per the R2 instruction, it is reported rather than
+faked — and it is not implementable honestly in this round for a second reason: a restructure of the
+production model-loading path cannot be validated here, because no model load, served run or bench is
+permitted. An unvalidated rewrite of `load_tensors()` labelled "closure" would be worse than this
+report.
+
+**Minimal architecture patch required (three parts):**
+
+1. `ggml/include/ggml-alloc.h` + `ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c` — expose the size census that the impl
+   already computes:
+   `GGML_API bool ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft_sizes(struct ggml_context * ctx, ggml_backend_buffer_type_t buft, size_t ** sizes, size_t * n_sizes);`
+   It runs the existing pass 1 (the `alloc_range` walk) with `no_alloc` semantics and returns the
+   per-buffer size list the caller must free. No behaviour change to any current caller.
+2. `ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c` — a plan-consuming variant:
+   `GGML_API ggml_backend_buffer_t ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft_planned(struct ggml_context * ctx, ggml_backend_buffer_type_t buft, ggml_backend_buffer_type_plan_t plan, size_t plan_base);`
+   `plan_base` is mandatory: one plan per buffer type now covers several contexts, so entry `i` of a
+   context maps to entry `plan_base + i` of that buffer type's plan. Without the offset the second
+   context of a buffer type would consume the first context's reservations.
+3. `src/llama-model.cpp:1748` — split the `ml.ctx_map` loop into two passes. Pass 1: for every
+   context that takes the allocation path (not `ml.no_alloc`, not the `buffer_from_host_ptr` mmap
+   path — Vulkan never takes the latter, `props.caps.buffer_from_host_ptr` is false), collect sizes
+   via (1), concatenate per buffer type, record each context's `plan_base`, and call
+   `ggml_backend_buft_plan_begin()` once per buffer type. If **any** buffer type answers INFEASIBLE
+   (or INDETERMINATE with more than one buffer), free every plan opened so far and throw before the
+   first driver allocation. Pass 2: allocate each context via (2), and on any failure free every plan
+   and every buffer built in this load. Buffer types that answer UNSUPPORTED keep the current
+   unplanned call, so CPU and every non-planning backend are untouched.
+
+That spans buffer-type groups and devices — each buffer type's plan reserves against its own device
+ledger, and every plan is open before the first plan-owned allocation. Scope note: this covers the
+model **tensor** load. KV cache and compute buffers are allocated later, during `llama_context`
+construction, and would need the same treatment to make a whole *serve* transaction; that is a
+separate boundary and is not claimed here. B7c bounded host splitting/staging remains out of scope
+and untouched.
+
+**A smaller truthful prerequisite commit is available** and is what the current tree is: the
+per-buffer-type plan API, the Vulkan batch planner with deterministic backtracking, the
+capacity precheck, the all-or-nothing INDETERMINATE contract, and the ABI version bump. It must be
+labelled *per-buffer-type batch allocation plan* — a B7b **prerequisite**, never B7b completion and
+never whole-load closure. The R3 text pass makes the in-code labels say exactly that.
