+---
+
+## 2. Design
+
+### 2.1 The ggml boundary (3 optional hooks, appended)
+
+`ggml_backend_buffer_type_i` gained three **optional** function pointers, appended at the end of the
+struct (`ggml/src/ggml-backend-impl.h`):
+
+```c
+ggml_backend_buffer_type_plan_t (*plan_begin)       (buft, const size_t * sizes, size_t n, enum ggml_backend_plan_status * status);
+ggml_backend_buffer_t           (*plan_alloc_buffer)(buft, plan, size_t i);
+void                            (*plan_free)        (buft, plan);
+```
+
+The plan object is backend-private (`struct ggml_backend_buffer_type_plan` is declared but never
+defined in shared code, so no two backends can collide on it).
+
+`plan_begin` answers with **three** outcomes, not two:
+
+| status | meaning | what ggml-alloc does |
+|---|---|---|
+| `FEASIBLE` | a complete assignment exists and is now reserved | allocate through the plan |
+| `INFEASIBLE` | no complete assignment exists | **fail the load before the first allocation** |
+| `INDETERMINATE` | the backend could not decide | allocate unplanned, exactly as before |
+| `UNSUPPORTED` | this buffer type has no `plan_begin` | allocate unplanned, exactly as before |
+
+The third outcome is load-bearing. A refusal that only means "I gave up deciding" would turn loads
+that work today into hard failures — the same reasoning the bulk-large-heap policy already carries at
+`ggml-vulkan.cpp` ("a policy that turns a working load into a hard failure is worse than the
+placement it is trying to fix"). The search bound and every un-measurable condition report
+INDETERMINATE, never INFEASIBLE.
+
+### 2.2 The Vulkan planner
+
+1. **Real requirements, not requested bytes.** `ggml_vk_buffer_memory_requirements()` returns the
+   driver's `VkMemoryRequirements` (size, alignment, `memoryTypeBits`) for a buffer built with the
+   *same* usage flags the allocator uses — those flags now come from one shared helper
+   (`ggml_vk_buffer_usage_flags`), so the planner cannot measure a different buffer shape than the
+   allocator builds. On this rig `VK_KHR_maintenance4` is available and the requirements are
+   obtained with **no VkBuffer object at all**; the fallback creates and immediately destroys a
+   VkBuffer, which is not memory. Receipt: `26-heapres-final-r1.txt` prints
+   `requirements via VK_KHR_maintenance4 (no VkBuffer created)`.
+
+2. **One definition of the placement policy.** `ggml_vk_placement_attempts()` is now the single
+   source of the attempt chain (bulk-large-heap `only_heap` attempt, then the
+   prefer_host / uma / disable_host_visible_vidmem / rebar branches). `ggml_vk_create_buffer_device()`
+   walks it and the planner walks it. Two hand-kept copies would drift and the plan would start
+   describing a placement nobody performs.
+
+3. **Candidates, in policy order.** For each buffer, candidates are built by
+   `ggml_vk_find_memory_properties()` — the *same* predicate the allocator uses for
+   `memoryTypeBits`, required property flags and `only_heap` — walked attempt by attempt and
+   deduplicated so a type satisfying two attempts keeps its first (preferred) position. The ledger
+   argument is now nullable; the planner passes `nullptr` because capacity for the whole set is
+   decided in one place instead.
+
+4. **Atomic whole-set reservation.** `vk_heap_ledger::reserve_plan()` searches for a complete
+   assignment and commits it **under the single ledger mutex**, so:
+   - two concurrent plans on one device cannot both be admitted against the same bytes;
+   - a single-buffer `reserve()` cannot slip in between deciding and charging;
+   - devices remain isolated, because the ledger is still a `vk_device_struct` member (B7a-R2).
+   Type indices and heap indices are separate fields throughout; the budget belongs to the **heap**.
+   Every capacity test is in subtraction form (`budget - reserved - delta`), so nothing can wrap.
+
+5. **Backtracking, with symmetry breaking.** Greedy first-choice is not sufficient: a buffer whose
+   only option is heap H must be able to push an earlier buffer off H. The walk backtracks. It also
+   exploits the fact that a real load hands the planner dozens of *interchangeable* buffers
+   (identical requirement, identical candidate list): for those, choices are restricted to
+   non-decreasing order, which is sound (any feasible assignment can be permuted into that canonical
+   form) and collapses what is otherwise 2^n arrangements of the same few real outcomes.
+
+### 2.3 Ownership — no double charge, exactly-once release
+
+`vk_heap_reservation::adopt()` (new, `noexcept`) takes ownership of bytes the plan **already**
+charged, without touching the ledger. `plan_alloc_buffer` adopts **first** and marks the entry
+consumed **immediately**, so the bytes are owned by exactly one thing at every instant: the plan
+before that line, the reservation after it. There is no window in which both, or neither, own them.
+
+`ggml_vk_create_buffer()` gained a `planned` branch: it does not search and does not charge, it
+moves the adopted reservation in and allocates the planned type. If the driver's requirement is
+larger than what the plan reserved it **fails closed** rather than allocating under-reserved.
+
+Failure paths, by construction:
+
+| what fails | who releases |
+|---|---|
+| plan infeasible | nothing was charged |
+| `allocateMemory` / map / bind / BDA / setup for buffer *i* | the adopted reservation, once, on unwind (B7a `vk_buffer_setup_guard` still frees the driver objects first) |
+| buffers already built | their own charge, once, from `~vk_buffer_struct` (B7a order: driver free first, ledger release last) |
+| entries never reached | `plan_free`, which releases **only unconsumed** entries |
+
+### 2.4 What was deliberately left alone
+
+Single-buffer callers (`ggml_vk_create_buffer_check`, prealloc scratch, `sync_staging`, the Escha
+LUT, every internal `ggml_vk_create_buffer` call) never enter the plan API and keep their exact
+behaviour. The imported-host-pointer path is untouched and still deliberately uncharged. The Vulkan
+**host** buffer type and the CPU fallback have no plan hooks, so they are `UNSUPPORTED` and behave
+exactly as before. `ggml_vk_create_buffer`'s `req_flags_list` parameter changed from
+`std::initializer_list` to `std::vector`; every existing `{a, b}` call site converts unchanged.
+
