+## 5. One real defect the tests found, not inspection
+
+The first run of the production test (`06-heapres.txt`, rc 1) failed on:
+
+```
+  FAIL impossible load: NOT ONE vkAllocateMemory was issued
+```
+
+with two `vkAllocateMemory` calls having actually happened. Cause: the impossible load is 35 buffers
+of 1 GiB, all with identical requirements and identical candidate lists over this rig's heaps. The
+exhaustive backtracking walk had to enumerate arrangements of interchangeable buffers, hit the search
+bound, returned INDETERMINATE, and the load fell through to the unplanned path — which allocated two
+buffers before failing. The planner was correct but **useless on any real model**, and a `buf == NULL`
+assertion alone would have reported that as a pass. The driver-allocation witness is what caught it.
+
+Fix: symmetry breaking (§2.2 item 5) plus a larger bound. Two new ledger assertions lock the
+behaviour in both directions — 40 interchangeable items over two heaps must be **decided**
+INFEASIBLE, and 12 must be FEASIBLE with the spill charged 8 MiB + 4 MiB exactly once each. The
+genuine worst case (30 items with *distinct* sizes, so symmetry breaking cannot apply, followed by an
+unplaceable item) still correctly reports INDETERMINATE rather than refusing.
+
+---
+
+## 6. What the tests prove, and the planted reds
+
+### Ledger half — `reserve_plan` on the test's own ledger
+
+| # | Assertion group |
+|---|---|
+| 1 | **candidate backtracking**: greedy would strand item 1; the walk undoes item 0 and both are placed |
+| 2 | **impossible plan** is refused and charges nothing (all-or-nothing) |
+| 3 | **shared heap types**: two different memory types on one heap share that heap's budget |
+| 4 | **separate heaps** stay independent |
+| 5 | **already-live reservations** are respected — a plan cannot spend bytes a live buffer holds |
+| 6 | **overflow** fails closed in both directions (`UINT64_MAX` item; `UINT64_MAX-1` already reserved) |
+| 7 | **out-of-range heap** fails closed |
+| 8 | **search bound** reports INDETERMINATE, never a refusal, and charges nothing |
+| 9 | **interchangeable items are decided**, not abandoned (the §5 regression, both directions) |
+
+### Production half — through `ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft`
+
+| # | Assertion group |
+|---|---|
+| 1 | **impossible complete plan**: refused, ledger untouched, and **not one `vkAllocateMemory`** was issued — keyed on the call counter, not on the NULL return |
+| 2 | **feasible load**: charged exactly once per buffer (a double charge or a lost charge both fail the bound), and destruction releases every byte exactly once |
+| 3 | **mid-plan failure**: a fault injected into the production allocator on the *second* buffer of three. The whole load fails, the counter proves it really got part way through, and the ledger returns exactly to its starting value — built buffer, failed buffer and untouched entries all |
+| 4 | **competing plans**: plan A takes three quarters of capacity, plan B is refused, B fits once A is released, and planning issued **zero** driver allocations |
+| 5 | the whole section leaves the device ledger exactly where it started |
+
+### Planted reds — all four fire at rc 1
+
+| Red | Planted defect | Failing assertions | rc |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| A | backtracking removed (greedy: refuse instead of undoing a choice) | all four backtracking checks, plus the search-bound check | 1 |
+| B | `plan_free` releases **consumed** entries too | `feasible load: charged exactly once per buffer, no double charge` | 1 |
+| C | `plan_alloc_buffer` charges again (`try_reserve`) instead of `adopt` | the exactly-once charge, the exactly-once release, the mid-plan rollback, and the section's closing ledger check | 1 |
+| D | `reserve_plan` ignores already-live reservations | the live-reservation refusal, the overflow-wrap guard, and `competing plans: plan B refused against plan A's reservations` | 1 |
